  <section>
    <h2>Approach</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Feature encoding.</strong> The features (cap shape, colour,
        gill attachment and so on) are all categorical, so I one-hot encoded
        them with Pandas and scikit-learn.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Model.</strong> A <code>DecisionTreeClassifier</code> with a
        capped depth, so the tree stays small enough to read.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Evaluation.</strong> Train/test splits and cross-validation to
        check it holds up on mushrooms it hasn't seen.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why a decision tree</h2>
    <p>
      When the cost of a wrong answer is someone getting poisoned, you want to
      be able to see why the model said "safe". A decision tree gives a clear
      path for each prediction, which a black-box model wouldn't.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Core of the model</h2>
    <pre
      class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-5 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg"><code>{`from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder
from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier

encoder = OneHotEncoder()
X_encoded = encoder.fit_transform(mushroom_features)

clf = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=5)
clf.fit(X_encoded, y_labels)

prediction = clf.predict(encoder.transform(new_sample))`}</code></pre>
  </section>
